: "0000-0001-9763-9609"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Kim Jaechul Graduate School of AI, Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology (KAIST), Daejeon, South Korea. jong.ye@kaist.ac.kr."}]}}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-7165-3373"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-9763-9609"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-1202-0880"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-8964-6275"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-9223-3172"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-1415-6471"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-4319-8435"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["supporting_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "1", "volume": "15", "journalIssueId": 3833425, "dateOfPublication": "2024 Oct", "monthOfPublication": 10, "yearOfPublication": 2024, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Nat Commun", "title": "Nature communications", "essn": "2041-1723", "issn": "2041-1723", "nlmid": "101528555", "isoabbreviation": "Nat Commun"}, "printPublicationDate": "2024-10-01"}, "pubYear": "2024", "pageInfo": "9186", "abstractText": "Target volume contouring for radiation therapy is considered significantly more challenging than the normal organ segmentation tasks as it necessitates the utilization of both image and text-based clinical information. Inspired by the recent advancement of large language models (LLMs) that can facilitate the integration of the textural information and images, here we present an LLM-driven multimodal artificial intelligence (AI), namely LLMSeg, that utilizes the clinical information and is applicable to the challenging task of 3-dimensional context-aware target volume delineation for radiation oncology. We validate our proposed LLMSeg within the context of breast cancer radiotherapy using external validation and data-insufficient environments, which attributes highly conducive to real-world applications. We demonstrate that the proposed multimodal LLMSeg exhibits markedly improved performance compared to conventional unimodal AI models, particularly exhibiting robust generalization performance and data-efficiency.", "affiliation": "Department of Radiology, Massachusetts General Hospital (MGH) and Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA.", "publicationStatus": "epublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't", "research-article", "Journal Article"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF)", "grantId": "RS-2023-00242164", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Research Foundation of Korea", "grantId": "RS-2024-00336454", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Research Foundation of Korea", "grantId": "RS-2023-00242164", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Research Foundation of Korea", "grantId": "RS-2024-00345854", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Research Foundation of Korea", "grantId": "No. 2022R1A2C2008623", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF)", "grantId": "RS-2024-00336454", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF)", "grantId": "RS-2023-00262527", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF)", "grantId": "No. 2022R1A2C2008623", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF)", "grantId": "RS-2024-00345854", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Research Foundation of Korea", "grantId": "RS-2023-00262527", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Humans"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Breast Neoplasms", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "RT", "qualifierName": "radiotherapy"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Tomography, X-Ray Computed"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Imaging, Three-Dimensional", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviati